IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `scr-dates`

-- Supreme Court (General Civil Procedure) Rules 2025 (Vic) -- the PARTY's questions about how a claim ends short of a
-- trial, and what offers to settle cost: Order 21 (default judgment), Order 22 (summary judgment: the times), Order 24
-- (dismissal for failing to prosecute or obey an order), Order 25 (discontinuance, and dismissal of an inactive
-- proceeding) and Order 26 Part 2 (offers of compromise and their costs consequences).
--
-- SEAT: the individual who is a party to a civil proceeding in the Supreme Court of Victoria, usually the plaintiff in a
-- personal injury claim, and in Order 26 either side of an offer: "Can I get judgment by default?", "Can my case be
-- dismissed?", "May I discontinue, and what will it cost me?", "Until when can I accept the offer?", "What costs follow
-- if my offer was not accepted?". The Court's orders, leave and judgments ("unreasonably", "willing and able", "genuine
-- compromise", "otherwise orders") are asked as facts.
--
-- ANSWERS as in scr-process.l4: JUST TRUE / JUST FALSE where the Rules say yes or no, NOTHING with a NOTHING-kind comment
-- where they do not answer. The Court is taken not to have ordered the Christmas vacation counted (r 3.04(1)) unless asked.
--
-- UNOFFICIAL VERSION: quotations from a plain-text conversion by Legalese Commons of the Word file of the Supreme Court
-- (General Civil Procedure) Rules 2025, S.R. No. 85/2025, Version No. 003 (1 January 2026), not the authorised version.
-- (c) State of Victoria; published with the authority of the Government Printer for the State of Victoria. No part may be
-- reproduced except in accordance with the Copyright Act 1968 (Cth). Quoted under the owner's ruling of 24 October 2026
-- (SOURCE-LICENSE.md).
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Supreme Court civil procedure: judgment without trial, ending a claim, and offers`

§§ `Order 21 -- judgment in default of appearance or defence`

DECLARE `The defendant's default` IS ONE OF
    `no appearance filed within the time`
    `no defence served within the time`
    `the defence was struck out`
    `no default`

DECLARE `What the claim is for` IS ONE OF
    `a debt`
    `damages`
    `the recovery of land`
    `the detention of goods`
    `other relief`

DECLARE `The judgment I may get by default` IS ONE OF
    `final judgment for the debt, with interest and costs`
    `interlocutory judgment for damages to be assessed, with costs`
    `judgment for possession of the land, with costs`
    `interlocutory judgment for the goods or their value to be assessed, with costs`
    `the Court may give judgment on the statement of claim`
    `no default judgment yet`

-- r 21.01(1): "This Rule applies only to a proceeding commenced by writ." (2): "Where a defendant does not file an
-- appearance within the time limited, the plaintiff may enter or apply for judgment ... unless Rule 2.07(1) of Chapter II
-- applies." (3): "Judgment shall not be entered or given for the plaintiff unless there is filed -- (a) an affidavit
-- proving service of the writ on the defendant; and (b) where the plaintiff applies for judgment in accordance with Rule
-- 21.04 and the indorsement of claim on the writ does not constitute a statement of claim ..., a statement of claim."
-- r 21.02(1): a defendant who, "being required to serve a defence, does not do so within the time limited"; (2): "unless an
-- affidavit proving the default is filed"; (3): a defence struck out. r 21.03(1): "(a) for the recovery of a debt, enter
-- final judgment ... together with interest ...; (b) for the recovery of damages, enter interlocutory judgment against that
-- defendant for the damages to be assessed; (c) for the recovery of land, enter judgment for possession ...; (d) for the
-- detention of goods, enter interlocutory judgment"; (1.2): not for a debt or damages "in a currency not Australian
-- dollars", where the Court may give judgment under r 21.04; (2): "the plaintiff may also enter judgment for costs".
-- r 21.04(1): other claims: "the Court may give judgment for the plaintiff upon the statement of claim". r 15.04: a
-- defendant under disability who has not appeared: "the plaintiff shall not continue the proceeding unless" a litigation
-- guardian is made or appointed.
-- No default, or a proceeding not begun by writ: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording* ("applies only to a proceeding
-- commenced by writ"; Order 21 speaks only of defaults).
@export Can I get judgment against the defendant because they did not file an appearance or serve a defence in time, and what judgment (rr 21.01 to 21.04, 15.04)? For a damages claim, interlocutory judgment for damages to be assessed (by an Associate Judge, Order 51) with costs, once the affidavit of service or of the default is filed. The Court may set a default judgment aside (r 21.07).
GIVEN `was my proceeding begun by writ` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your proceeding was commenced by writ
      `what is the default` IS A `The defendant's default` @desc The defendant's default: no appearance filed within the time, no defence served within the time, the defence struck out, or no default
      `is the affidavit filed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the affidavit proving service of the writ (for no appearance) or proving the default (for no defence) has been filed
      `is a statement of claim filed or on the writ` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the writ's claim is headed "Statement of Claim", or a statement of claim has been filed (needed where you apply to the Court for judgment on other relief)
      `is a Commercial List Judge managing the case` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the case is in a Commercial List managed by a Commercial List Judge (then that Judge's order is needed first)
      `is the defendant a minor or under disability without a guardian` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant is a minor or unable to manage their affairs and has no litigation guardian
      `what is the claim for` IS A `What the claim is for` @desc What the claim is for: a debt, damages (for example for an injury), the recovery of land, the detention of goods, or other relief
      `is it in Australian dollars` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a debt or damages: whether the amount is claimed in Australian dollars
GIVETH A MAYBE `The judgment I may get by default`
`What judgment can I get by default` MEANS
    IF (NOT `was my proceeding begun by writ`)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE CONSIDER `what is the default`
         WHEN `no default` THEN NOTHING
         OTHERWISE
            IF `is a Commercial List Judge managing the case` OR `is the defendant a minor or under disability without a guardian` OR (NOT `is the affidavit filed`)
            THEN JUST `no default judgment yet`
            ELSE CONSIDER `what is the claim for`
                 WHEN `a debt` THEN `the debt or damages judgment` `final judgment for the debt, with interest and costs`
                 WHEN `damages` THEN `the debt or damages judgment` `interlocutory judgment for damages to be assessed, with costs`
                 WHEN `the recovery of land` THEN JUST `judgment for possession of the land, with costs`
                 WHEN `the detention of goods` THEN JUST `interlocutory judgment for the goods or their value to be assessed, with costs`
                 WHEN `other relief` THEN `the Court's judgment on the statement of claim`
    WHERE
        `the Court's judgment on the statement of claim` MEANS
            IF `is a statement of claim filed or on the writ`
            THEN JUST `the Court may give judgment on the statement of claim`
            ELSE JUST `no default judgment yet`
        `the debt or damages judgment` j MEANS
            IF `is it in Australian dollars`
            THEN JUST j
            ELSE `the Court's judgment on the statement of claim`

§§ `Order 22 -- summary judgment: the times for service`

-- r 22.04(4): "The plaintiff shall serve the summons and a copy of the affidavit or affidavits and of any exhibit ... on the
-- defendant not less than 14 days before the day for hearing named in the summons." r 22.18(4): the same for a defendant's
-- application. Counted back from the hearing (that day excluded), with r 3.04 (the Christmas vacation excluded) and r
-- 3.01(5) (fork F10: a last day on a weekend moves to the next open day, as the rule reads).
@export By what day must I serve my summary judgment summons and supporting affidavits on the other side (rr 22.04(4), 22.18(4))? Not less than 14 days before the hearing day named in the summons, whether I am the plaintiff or the defendant applying.
GIVEN `the day of the hearing` IS A DATE @desc The day for hearing named in the summons, as an ISO date
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must my summary judgment application be served` MEANS
    `r 3.01(5) -- the day the act may be done for a last day of` (`the last counted day` 14 `days before` `the day of the hearing` FALSE) FALSE

-- r 22.05(3): "Unless the Court otherwise orders, the defendant shall serve a copy of any affidavit and of any exhibit ...
-- on the plaintiff not less than three days before the day for hearing named in the summons." r 22.19(3): the same for a
-- plaintiff answering a defendant's application. Three days is "5 days or less": Saturdays and Sundays are not counted
-- (r 3.01(4)).
-- The Court having ordered otherwise: NOTHING-kind *points outside the text*.
@export By what day must I serve my affidavit opposing the other side's summary judgment application (rr 22.05(3), 22.19(3))? Not less than three days before the hearing day (weekends not counted), unless the Court orders otherwise.
GIVEN `the day of the hearing` IS A DATE @desc The day for hearing named in the summons, as an ISO date
      `has the Court ordered another time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has ordered a different time (answer no if not)
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`By when must my affidavit against summary judgment be served` MEANS
    IF `has the Court ordered another time`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST (`r 3.01(5) -- the day the act may be done for a last day of` (`the last counted day` 3 `days before` `the day of the hearing` FALSE) FALSE)

-- r 22.15 and r 22.23: "The Court may set aside or vary any judgment given against a party who does not attend on the
-- hearing of an application under section 61 [or 62] of the Civil Procedure Act 2010."
-- A party who attended: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording* (only an appeal or another power).
@export If summary judgment was given against me when I did not attend the hearing, can the Court set it aside (rr 22.15, 22.23)?
GIVEN `did I attend the hearing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you (or your lawyer) attended the hearing of the summary judgment application
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the summary judgment against me be set aside` MEANS
    IF `did I attend the hearing`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST TRUE

§§ `Order 24 -- failure to prosecute or to obey an order`

-- r 24.01: "The Court may order that a proceeding be dismissed for want of prosecution if the plaintiff -- (a) being
-- required to serve a statement of claim, fails to do so within the time limited; or (b) does not within a reasonable time
-- after the commencement of the proceeding -- (i) file and serve notice of trial; or (ii) apply to have a date fixed for the
-- trial of the proceeding; or (c) fails to file and serve notice of trial within the time allowed to the plaintiff by the
-- Court when fixing a date for the trial of the proceeding under Rule 48.02(b)." r 48.04: the defendant may then file and
-- serve notice of trial, or apply to dismiss. r 24.05: the Court's inherent power to dismiss is not affected.
-- None of (a) to (c): NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording* (the inherent power remains, r 24.05).
@export Can my proceeding be dismissed for want of prosecution (rr 24.01, 48.04)? The Court may dismiss it if I did not serve my statement of claim in time, did not within a reasonable time file and serve notice of trial or apply for a trial date, or missed the time the Court gave me for notice of trial.
GIVEN `did I miss the time for my statement of claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you had to serve a statement of claim and did not do so within the time limited
      `has a reasonable time passed without notice of trial or a trial date application` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a reasonable time has passed since the case began without you filing and serving notice of trial or applying for a trial date (a judgment the Court makes; answer as you expect the Court to see it)
      `did I miss the Court's time for notice of trial` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court fixed a trial date subject to your filing and serving notice of trial within a time, and you did not
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can my proceeding be dismissed for want of prosecution` MEANS
    IF `did I miss the time for my statement of claim` OR `has a reasonable time passed without notice of trial or a trial date application` OR `did I miss the Court's time for notice of trial`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING

DECLARE `My side of the case` IS ONE OF
    `plaintiff`
    `defendant`

DECLARE `What can follow my default` IS ONE OF
    `my proceeding may be dismissed`
    `my defence may be struck out, and judgment in default of defence may follow`

-- r 24.02(1): "Where a party fails to comply with an order to give particulars of any pleading or with an order for the
-- discovery or inspection of documents or for answers to interrogatories, the Court may order -- (a) if the party is the
-- plaintiff, that the proceeding be dismissed; (b) if the party is a defendant, that the defendant's defence, if any, be
-- struck out." (2): a defendant whose defence is struck out is treated as in default of defence (r 21.02(1)).
-- No failure to comply: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording*.
@export What can happen to my case if I do not obey an order to give particulars, to give discovery or inspection of documents, or to answer interrogatories (r 24.02)? A plaintiff's proceeding may be dismissed; a defendant's defence may be struck out, and default judgment may follow.
GIVEN `my side` IS A `My side of the case` @desc Whether you are the plaintiff or a defendant
      `have I failed to comply with such an order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have failed to comply with an order for particulars, discovery or inspection of documents, or answers to interrogatories
GIVETH A MAYBE `What can follow my default`
`What can follow my failure to obey the order` MEANS
    IF (NOT `have I failed to comply with such an order`)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE CONSIDER `my side`
         WHEN `plaintiff` THEN JUST `my proceeding may be dismissed`
         WHEN `defendant` THEN JUST `my defence may be struck out, and judgment in default of defence may follow`

-- r 24.03: "Where -- (a) a proceeding is dismissed for want of prosecution and the plaintiff is liable to pay the costs of
-- the defendant of the proceeding; and (b) the plaintiff, before paying those costs commences another proceeding for the
-- same, or substantially the same, cause of action -- the Court may by order stay the proceeding until those costs are
-- paid." r 25.07: the same where a party is liable for costs "by reason of a discontinuance or a withdrawal".
-- Otherwise: JUST FALSE (the "where" conditions fail; ruling (b)).
@export If my earlier case was dismissed for want of prosecution, or I discontinued it, and I have not paid the costs I owe from it, can my new case on the same claim be stayed until I pay (rr 24.03, 25.07)?
GIVEN `was my earlier case dismissed for want of prosecution or discontinued` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your earlier proceeding was dismissed for want of prosecution, or you discontinued or withdrew it
      `do I owe costs from it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are liable to pay the other party's costs of that earlier proceeding
      `have I paid them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have paid those costs
      `is the new case for substantially the same cause of action` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the new proceeding is for the same, or substantially the same, cause of action
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can my new case be stayed until I pay the old costs` MEANS
    IF `was my earlier case dismissed for want of prosecution or discontinued` AND `do I owe costs from it` AND (NOT `have I paid them`) AND `is the new case for substantially the same cause of action`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `Order 25 -- discontinuance, withdrawal, and inactive proceedings`

-- r 25.02(1): "This Rule applies only to a proceeding commenced by writ." (2): "A plaintiff may discontinue a proceeding or
-- withdraw any part of it -- (a) before the close of pleadings; or (b) at any time, by leave of the Court or with the consent
-- of all other parties." (3): the same for a defendant's counterclaim. r 25.03: a proceeding not commenced by writ: "at any
-- time -- (a) by leave of the Court; or (b) with the consent of all other parties." r 25.04(1): a discontinuance without
-- leave is made "by filing a notice"; (2) indorsed with the consent of each consenting party; (3) served on each other
-- party the same day.
-- Outside (a) and (b) without leave: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording* (a grant limited by its own terms; fork
-- F11: the other reading is JUST FALSE).
@export May I discontinue my case (or my counterclaim), or withdraw part of it, without the Court's leave (rr 25.02 to 25.04)? In a case begun by writ, yes before the pleadings close, and at any time with the consent of all the other parties; otherwise only with leave. Without leave it is done by filing a notice and serving a copy on each other party the same day.
GIVEN `was my proceeding begun by writ` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your proceeding was commenced by writ
      `have the pleadings closed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the pleadings have closed (30 days after the defence or the last pleading, r 14.08)
      `do all the other parties consent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether all the other parties (to the counterclaim, if it is your counterclaim) consent in writing
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May I discontinue without leave` MEANS
    IF `do all the other parties consent`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE IF `was my proceeding begun by writ` AND (NOT `have the pleadings closed`)
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE NOTHING

-- r 25.05: costs on a discontinuance or withdrawal "shall be determined in accordance with Rule 63.15". r 63.15: "Unless the
-- Court otherwise orders, a party who discontinues or withdraws part of a proceeding, counterclaim or claim by third party
-- notice shall pay the costs of the party to whom the discontinuance or withdrawal relates to the time of the
-- discontinuance or withdrawal."
@export If I discontinue my case or withdraw part of it, must I pay the other party's costs (rr 25.05, 63.15)? Yes, their costs up to the discontinuance or withdrawal, unless the Court orders otherwise.
GIVEN `has the Court ordered otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has made a different order about the costs of the discontinuance
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must I pay costs if I discontinue` MEANS
    IF `has the Court ordered otherwise`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE JUST TRUE

-- r 25.06: "The discontinuance of a proceeding ... or the withdrawal of any part ... shall not be a defence to a subsequent
-- proceeding for the same, or substantially the same, cause of action, unless the Court otherwise provides by any order
-- granting leave to discontinue or withdraw."
@export If I discontinue my case, can the defendant rely on the discontinuance as a defence to a new case on the same claim (r 25.06)? No, unless the Court's order giving leave to discontinue says so. (The limitation period still runs.)
GIVEN `did the leave order provide that it is a defence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court's order giving leave to discontinue or withdraw provides that the discontinuance is a defence to a later proceeding
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my discontinuance a defence to a new case` MEANS
    IF `did the leave order provide that it is a defence`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

DECLARE `What happens to my inactive case` IS ONE OF
    `not yet open to a dismissal notice`
    `open to a dismissal notice from the Prothonotary`
    `a party may still file a notice of intention to continue`
    `kept alive by a notice of intention to continue`
    `dismissed without adjudication on the merits`

-- r 25.08(1): "Where a year or more has elapsed since any party has taken any step in a proceeding, and no party desiring the
-- proceeding to continue has given notice in accordance with Rule 3.06, the Prothonotary may send a notice to all parties
-- ... stating that -- ... (b) the parties have 30 days from the date the notice is sent to file in RedCrest a notice of
-- intention to continue the proceeding; and (c) if no notice of intention is filed ..., the proceeding will be dismissed
-- without further notice." (2): "If no notice is filed in RedCrest by any party in accordance with paragraph (1), the
-- proceeding is dismissed without adjudication on its merits." (3): the party whose proceeding is dismissed pays the
-- other parties' costs unless the Court orders otherwise. (4): the Court may reinstate it.
GIVEN `the day the Prothonotary sent the notice` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`r 25.08 -- the last day for a notice of intention` `the day the Prothonotary sent the notice` MEANS
    `r 3.01(5) -- the day the act may be done for a last day of` (`the last counted day of` 30 `days after` `the day the Prothonotary sent the notice` FALSE) FALSE

@export Is my inactive case at risk of being dismissed by the Prothonotary, or has it been (r 25.08)? After a year with no step by anyone and no month's notice under rule 3.06, the Prothonotary may send a notice; unless a party files a notice of intention to continue within 30 days, the case is dismissed (without a decision on the merits), and the plaintiff pays the other parties' costs unless the Court orders otherwise. The Court may reinstate it.
GIVEN `the day of the last step by any party` IS A DATE @desc The day any party last took a step in the proceeding, as an ISO date
      `has a party given notice under rule 3.06` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a party has given every other party a month's written notice that it wants the case to continue
      `has the Prothonotary sent a dismissal notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Prothonotary has sent the parties a notice under rule 25.08
      `the day the Prothonotary sent it` IS A DATE @desc If sent: the day the Prothonotary sent it (any date if not)
      `has a party filed a notice of intention to continue` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether any party has filed in RedCrest a notice of intention to continue the proceeding
      `the day it was filed` IS A DATE @desc If filed: the day it was filed (any date if not)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `What happens to my inactive case`
`What happens to my inactive case now` MEANS
    IF `has the Prothonotary sent a dismissal notice`
    THEN IF `has a party filed a notice of intention to continue` AND `the day it was filed` `is on or before` `the last day`
         THEN `kept alive by a notice of intention to continue`
         ELSE IF `today` `is on or before` `the last day`
              THEN `a party may still file a notice of intention to continue`
              ELSE `dismissed without adjudication on the merits`
    ELSE IF (NOT `has a party given notice under rule 3.06`) AND `today` `is after` (`the last counted day of` 12 `months after` `the day of the last step by any party` FALSE)
         THEN `open to a dismissal notice from the Prothonotary`
         ELSE `not yet open to a dismissal notice`
    WHERE
        `the last day` MEANS `r 25.08 -- the last day for a notice of intention` `the day the Prothonotary sent it`

@export By what day must a notice of intention to continue be filed after the Prothonotary's inactivity notice (r 25.08(1)(b))? Within 30 days from the date the notice is sent.
GIVEN `the day the Prothonotary sent the notice` IS A DATE @desc The day the Prothonotary sent the notice under rule 25.08, as an ISO date
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must the notice of intention to continue be filed` MEANS
    `r 25.08 -- the last day for a notice of intention` `the day the Prothonotary sent the notice`

§§ `Order 26 Part 2 -- offers of compromise`

-- r 26.02(3): "An offer of compromise must -- (a) be in writing and prepared in accordance with Rules 27.02 to 27.04; and (b)
-- contain a statement to the effect that it is served in accordance with this Order." (4): "An offer of compromise must
-- state either -- (a) that the offer is inclusive of costs; or (b) that costs are to be paid or received ... in addition to
-- the offer." r 26.03(3): a time limit stated "shall not be less than 14 days after such service".
@export Is my offer to settle in the form an Order 26 offer of compromise needs, so that its costs consequences can apply (rr 26.02, 26.03(3))? It must be in writing in the Court's document form, say it is served under Order 26, say whether it includes costs or costs are extra, and, if it is open for a limited time, give at least 14 days after service.
GIVEN `is it in writing in the Court's document form` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offer is in writing and prepared as rules 27.02 to 27.04 require (the form of court documents)
      `does it say it is served under Order 26` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it contains a statement to the effect that it is served in accordance with Order 26
      `does it say whether costs are included or extra` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it states either that it is inclusive of costs, or that costs are to be paid or received in addition
      `is it limited in time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it states a time after service within which it may be accepted
      `how many days it is open` IS A NUMBER @desc If limited in time: the number of days after service it is open (0 if not limited)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my offer of compromise in proper form` MEANS
    IF      `is it in writing in the Court's document form`
        AND `does it say it is served under Order 26`
        AND `does it say whether costs are included or extra`
        AND (NOT (`is it limited in time` AND `how many days it is open` LESS THAN 14))
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- r 26.03(4): "A party on whom an offer of compromise is served may accept the offer by serving notice of acceptance in
-- writing on the party who made the offer before -- (a) the expiration of the time specified in accordance with paragraph
-- (3) or, if no time is specified, the expiration of 14 days after service of the offer; or (b) verdict or judgment in
-- respect of the claim to which the offer relates -- whichever event is the sooner." A stated time of less than 14 days is
-- read as 14 (fork F12). Acceptance must come on a day before the day of verdict or judgment (fork F13). r 3.01(5) moves a
-- last day on a weekend or public holiday, but not past the verdict.
GIVEN `the day the offer was served` IS A DATE
      `the offer states a time`           IS A BOOLEAN
      `the days it states`                IS A NUMBER
      `there has been a verdict or judgment` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day of the verdict or judgment` IS A DATE
      `that last day is a public holiday` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DATE
`r 26.03(4) -- the last day to accept` `the day the offer was served` `the offer states a time` `the days it states` `there has been a verdict or judgment` `the day of the verdict or judgment` `that last day is a public holiday` MEANS
    IF `there has been a verdict or judgment` AND `the day of the verdict or judgment` `is on or before` `the last day of the time`
    THEN `the date` (0 MINUS 1) `days after` `the day of the verdict or judgment`
    ELSE `the last day of the time`
    WHERE
        `the days open` MEANS
            IF `the offer states a time`
            THEN max `the days it states` 14
            ELSE 14
        `the last day of the time` MEANS `r 3.01(5) -- the day the act may be done for a last day of` (`the last counted day of` `the days open` `days after` `the day the offer was served` FALSE) `that last day is a public holiday`

@export Until what day can I accept an offer of compromise served on me (r 26.03(3), (4))? By serving written notice of acceptance before the time the offer states (at least 14 days after service) or, if it states none, before 14 days after service, and in any case before verdict or judgment. The offer cannot be withdrawn while it is open, unless the Court orders (r 26.03(5)).
GIVEN `the day the offer was served on me` IS A DATE @desc The day the offer of compromise was served on you, as an ISO date
      `does the offer state a time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offer states a time within which it may be accepted
      `how many days the offer states` IS A NUMBER @desc If so: the number of days after service it states (0 if none)
      `has there been a verdict or judgment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a verdict or judgment has been given (or a date for it is known) on the claim the offer relates to
      `the day of the verdict or judgment` IS A DATE @desc If so: its day (any date otherwise)
      `is that last day a public holiday` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the day the time would otherwise end on is a public holiday in Victoria (answer no if unsure)
GIVETH A DATE
`Until when can I accept the offer` MEANS
    `r 26.03(4) -- the last day to accept` `the day the offer was served on me` `does the offer state a time` `how many days the offer states` `has there been a verdict or judgment` `the day of the verdict or judgment` `is that last day a public holiday`

@export Can I still accept the offer of compromise served on me today (r 26.03(4))? Outside the time the answer is no; the offer may still be relevant to costs (r 26.08).
GIVEN `the day the offer was served on me` IS A DATE @desc The day the offer of compromise was served on you, as an ISO date
      `does the offer state a time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offer states a time within which it may be accepted
      `how many days the offer states` IS A NUMBER @desc If so: the number of days after service it states (0 if none)
      `has there been a verdict or judgment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a verdict or judgment has been given on the claim the offer relates to
      `the day of the verdict or judgment` IS A DATE @desc If so: its day (any date otherwise)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date, or the day you would serve the acceptance
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I still accept the offer` MEANS
    IF `today` `is on or before` (`r 26.03(4) -- the last day to accept` `the day the offer was served on me` `does the offer state a time` `how many days the offer states` `has there been a verdict or judgment` `the day of the verdict or judgment` FALSE)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- r 26.03.1: "An offer of compromise providing for payment of a specified sum of money to a party shall, unless it otherwise
-- provides, be taken to be an offer providing for payment of that sum within 28 days after acceptance of the offer."
-- The offer providing its own time: NOTHING-kind *points outside the text* (the offer's terms).
GIVEN `the day of acceptance` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`r 26.03.1 -- the last day for payment` `the day of acceptance` MEANS
    `r 3.01(5) -- the day the act may be done for a last day of` (`the last counted day of` 28 `days after` `the day of acceptance` FALSE) FALSE

@export If I accept an offer to pay me a sum, by what day must it be paid (r 26.03.1)? Within 28 days after acceptance, unless the offer says otherwise.
GIVEN `the day I accepted` IS A DATE @desc The day you served your notice of acceptance, as an ISO date
      `does the offer set its own time for payment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offer itself provides when the sum is to be paid
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`By when must the accepted sum be paid` MEANS
    IF `does the offer set its own time for payment`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST (`r 26.03.1 -- the last day for payment` `the day I accepted`)

-- r 26.07(1): "A party who has accepted an offer for the payment to that party of a sum of money may withdraw the
-- acceptance if -- (a) the sum of money is not paid -- (i) within the time provided by the offer; or (ii) where no time is
-- specified by the offer, within 28 days after acceptance of the offer; and (b) the Court, on the application of the party
-- who accepted the offer, gives leave."
@export I accepted an offer to pay me money and it was not paid in time: may I withdraw my acceptance (r 26.07)? Only if it was not paid within the offer's time (or 28 days after acceptance) and the Court gives leave; the Court may also restore the parties' positions.
GIVEN `was it paid within the time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the sum was paid within the time the offer provides or, if none, within 28 days after your acceptance
      `has the Court given leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court, on your application, has given leave to withdraw the acceptance
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May I withdraw my acceptance` MEANS
    IF (NOT `was it paid within the time`) AND `has the Court given leave`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- r 26.07.1: "If, after acceptance of an offer of compromise, a party to the accepted offer defaults in complying with that
-- party's obligations under the offer, any non-defaulting party ... may apply to the Court for an order -- (a) giving effect
-- to the accepted offer; (b) staying or dismissing the proceeding if the plaintiff is in default; (c) striking out the
-- defendant's defence if the defendant is in default; or (d) that a claim, not the subject of the offer, shall proceed."
-- r 26.07.2(1): it "does not apply if -- (a) two or more defendants are alleged to be jointly, or jointly and severally,
-- liable to the plaintiff for a debt or damages; and (b) rights of contribution or indemnity appear to exist between the
-- defendants." (2): but it applies if the offer was to or by all defendants to compromise the claim against all of them
-- (and, where made by two or more, they offer to be jointly or jointly and severally liable for the whole).
-- No default by the other party: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording*.
@export The other side has not done what our accepted offer of compromise requires: can I apply to the Court to enforce it, or to stay, dismiss or strike out (r 26.07.1, 26.07.2)? Yes, except where two or more defendants are said to be jointly liable with rights of contribution between them, unless the offer compromised the claim against all of them.
GIVEN `has the other party defaulted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the other party to the accepted offer has failed to do what the offer requires of it (for example to pay)
      `are two or more defendants said to be jointly liable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether two or more defendants are alleged to be jointly, or jointly and severally, liable to the plaintiff for the debt or damages
      `do contribution rights appear to exist between them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether rights of contribution or indemnity appear to exist between those defendants
      `was the offer to compromise the claim against all defendants` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offer was made to (or by) all the defendants to compromise the claim against all of them, with any defendants offering together being jointly liable for the whole
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I apply to enforce the accepted offer` MEANS
    IF (NOT `has the other party defaulted`)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `are two or more defendants said to be jointly liable` AND `do contribution rights appear to exist between them` AND (NOT `was the offer to compromise the claim against all defendants`)
         THEN JUST FALSE
         ELSE JUST TRUE

-- r 26.05(1): "No statement of the fact that an offer of compromise has been made shall be contained in any pleading or
-- affidavit." (2): an unaccepted offer: "no communication with respect to the offer shall be made to the Court on the trial
-- of the proceeding until after all questions of liability and the relief to be granted have been determined" (except
-- the fact and date of service, for interest, r 26.08(6)). (3): not where the offer says it is not made without
-- prejudice.
@export May my unaccepted offer of compromise be mentioned to the judge at trial (r 26.05)? Not until liability and the relief have been decided, unless the offer says it is not made without prejudice.
GIVEN `does the offer say it is not without prejudice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offer says it is not made without prejudice (offers are without prejudice unless they say otherwise)
      `have liability and the relief been decided` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether all questions of liability and the relief to be granted have been decided at the trial
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May my offer be mentioned to the trial judge` MEANS
    IF `does the offer say it is not without prejudice` OR `have liability and the relief been decided`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

DECLARE `Who made the offer` IS ONE OF
    `the plaintiff`
    `the defendant`

DECLARE `The costs order the offer brings` IS ONE OF
    `the plaintiff's costs on the indemnity basis`
    `the plaintiff's costs on the standard basis to 11 am on the second business day after service, and on the indemnity basis after`
    `the plaintiff's costs on the standard basis to 11 am on the second business day after service, and the defendant's costs on the standard basis after`
    `the defendant's costs on the standard basis to 11 am on the second business day after the offer, and on the indemnity basis after`
    `no costs order under rule 26.08`

-- r 26.01: "ordinarily applicable basis means -- (a) in respect of costs incurred before 1 April 2013, party and party basis;
-- (b) in respect of costs incurred on or after 1 April 2013, standard basis" (costs before 2013 are not asked: the answers
-- say "standard basis").
-- r 26.08(1): an offer "which has not been accepted at the time of verdict or judgment". (2): "Where an offer of compromise
-- is made by a plaintiff and not accepted by the defendant, and the plaintiff obtains a judgment on the claim ... no less
-- favourable to the plaintiff than the terms of the offer, then, unless the Court otherwise orders, the plaintiff shall be
-- entitled -- (a) if the claim of the plaintiff is for damages for or arising out of death or bodily injury, to an order
-- against the defendant for the plaintiff's costs in respect of the claim taxed on an indemnity basis; (b) in the case of
-- any other claim ..., [costs] before 11.00 a.m. on the second business day after the offer was served, taxed on the
-- ordinarily applicable basis and ... thereafter taxed on an indemnity basis." (3): a defendant's offer not accepted, and
-- a judgment "not more favourable to the plaintiff than the terms of the offer": the plaintiff's costs to 11 am on the
-- second business day after service, ordinarily applicable basis; the defendant's costs thereafter, the same basis. (4): a
-- defendant's offer the plaintiff "unreasonably fails to accept", and the claim dismissed or judgment for the defendant:
-- the defendant's costs "until 11.00 a.m. on the second business day after the offer was made" on the ordinarily
-- applicable basis, and after that on an indemnity basis. (5): interest "as relates to the period after the day the offer
-- of compromise was served" is disregarded in comparing. (7): (2) to (4) "shall not apply unless the Court is satisfied by
-- the party serving the offer ... that that party was at all material times willing and able to carry out" its part. (8):
-- where only liability was in dispute, (2) "shall not apply unless the Court is satisfied that the plaintiff's offer was of
-- a genuine compromise".
-- The amounts are compared as money for the plaintiff, the judgment net of post-offer interest, on the same footing as to
-- costs as the offer (fork F14). Outside (2) to (4): the Court's ordinary discretion (r 63.02; "no costs order under rule
-- 26.08").
@export My offer of compromise (or the other side's) was not accepted and the claim has gone to judgment: what costs order does rule 26.08 give? A plaintiff who beats (or equals) its own offer in an injury claim gets all its costs on the indemnity basis; a plaintiff who does no better than the defendant's offer pays the defendant's costs from 11 am on the second business day after it was served; a plaintiff who loses after unreasonably refusing the defendant's offer pays indemnity costs from then. The Court can order otherwise.
GIVEN `who made the offer` IS A `Who made the offer` @desc Who made the unaccepted offer of compromise: the plaintiff or the defendant
      `was it accepted before verdict or judgment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offer was accepted before verdict or judgment (if so, rule 26.08 does not apply)
      `is it a death or bodily injury damages claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the plaintiff's claim is for damages for or arising out of death or bodily injury
      `the amount offered` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount the offer would have paid the plaintiff, in dollars, on the same footing as to costs as the judgment is compared
      `did the plaintiff obtain judgment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the plaintiff obtained judgment on the claim (answer no if the claim was dismissed or judgment was for the defendant)
      `the judgment amount` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount of the plaintiff's judgment in dollars (0 if none)
      `the interest in it after the offer was served` IS A NUMBER @desc The part of the judgment that is interest (or damages in the nature of interest) for the period after the day the offer was served, in dollars (disregarded in comparing)
      `did the plaintiff unreasonably fail to accept` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a defendant's offer, where the plaintiff lost: whether the Court finds the plaintiff unreasonably failed to accept it
      `was the offeror willing and able throughout` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied that the party who made the offer was at all material times willing and able to carry it out
      `was only liability in dispute` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the amount of the damages was not in dispute, only liability
      `was the plaintiff's offer a genuine compromise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If only liability was in dispute: whether the Court is satisfied that the plaintiff's offer was a genuine compromise
      `has the Court ordered otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has ordered otherwise
GIVETH A `The costs order the offer brings`
`What costs order follows the unaccepted offer` MEANS
    IF `was it accepted before verdict or judgment` OR `has the Court ordered otherwise` OR (NOT `was the offeror willing and able throughout`)
    THEN `no costs order under rule 26.08`
    ELSE CONSIDER `who made the offer`
         WHEN `the plaintiff` THEN
            IF `did the plaintiff obtain judgment` AND `the amount compared` AT LEAST `the amount offered` AND (NOT (`was only liability in dispute` AND (NOT `was the plaintiff's offer a genuine compromise`)))
            THEN IF `is it a death or bodily injury damages claim`
                 THEN `the plaintiff's costs on the indemnity basis`
                 ELSE `the plaintiff's costs on the standard basis to 11 am on the second business day after service, and on the indemnity basis after`
            ELSE `no costs order under rule 26.08`
         WHEN `the defendant` THEN
            IF `did the plaintiff obtain judgment`
            THEN IF `the amount compared` AT MOST `the amount offered`
                 THEN `the plaintiff's costs on the standard basis to 11 am on the second business day after service, and the defendant's costs on the standard basis after`
                 ELSE `no costs order under rule 26.08`
            ELSE IF `did the plaintiff unreasonably fail to accept`
                 THEN `the defendant's costs on the standard basis to 11 am on the second business day after the offer, and on the indemnity basis after`
                 ELSE `no costs order under rule 26.08`
    WHERE
        `the amount compared` MEANS `the judgment amount` MINUS `the interest in it after the offer was served`

-- "11.00 a.m. on the second business day after the offer was served" (r 26.08(2)(b), (3), (4); r 26.10). r 26.01: business
-- day, a day the office is open (r 3.08).
@export From what day (at 11 am) does the costs consequence of an offer of compromise switch (rr 26.08, 26.01)? 11 am on the second business day (a day the Court's office is open) after the offer was served.
GIVEN `the day the offer was served` IS A DATE @desc The day the offer of compromise was served, as an ISO date
      `does a public holiday fall in the next two weekdays` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a Victorian public holiday falls on either of the two weekdays after that day (answer no if unsure)
GIVETH A DATE
`From when do the offer's costs consequences run` MEANS
    `the second business day after` `the day the offer was served` `does a public holiday fall in the next two weekdays`

-- r 26.08.1(1): "If -- (a) a party, before a proceeding has commenced, has made an offer in writing to another party (whether
-- or not expressed to be without prejudice) to compromise any claim made in the proceeding on the terms specified in the
-- offer; and (b) the offer was open to be accepted for a reasonable time, but was not accepted; and (c) the offeror obtains
-- an order or judgment in respect of the claim no less favourable to the offeror than the terms of the offer -- the Court
-- shall take those matters into account in determining what order for costs to make". (2): it may order costs on another
-- basis, from the day of the offer, the commencement, or another time.
-- The conditions not all met: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording* (the Court's general discretion remains).
@export I made a written offer to settle before I started my case and it was refused: must the Court take it into account on costs (r 26.08.1)? Yes, if it was open for a reasonable time and my result is no less favourable to me than the offer; the Court may then order the other side to pay my costs on a higher basis.
GIVEN `was the offer in writing before the case began` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you made the offer in writing (with or without prejudice) before the proceeding was commenced, to compromise a claim made in it
      `was it open for a reasonable time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offer was open to be accepted for a reasonable time (a judgment the Court makes)
      `was it accepted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the other party accepted it
      `is my result no less favourable to me than the offer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the order or judgment you obtained is no less favourable to you than the terms of your offer
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must the Court consider my pre-litigation offer` MEANS
    IF `was the offer in writing before the case began` AND `was it open for a reasonable time` AND (NOT `was it accepted`) AND `is my result no less favourable to me than the offer`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING
